🚀 𝟑𝟎 𝐃𝐚𝐲𝐬 𝐨𝐟 𝐀𝐧𝐬𝐢𝐛𝐥𝐞 – 𝐃𝐚𝐲 𝟏𝟕/𝟑𝟎

🧩 𝐀𝐧𝐬𝐢𝐛𝐥𝐞 𝐉𝐢𝐧𝐣𝐚𝟐 𝐓𝐞𝐦𝐩𝐥𝐚𝐭𝐞𝐬 – 𝐌𝐚𝐤𝐞 𝐂𝐨𝐧𝐟𝐢𝐠𝐮𝐫𝐚𝐭𝐢𝐨𝐧 𝐅𝐢𝐥𝐞𝐬 𝐃𝐲𝐧𝐚𝐦𝐢𝐜

What if the same application needs different configuration values in Development, QA, and Production?

Maintaining separate configuration files for every environment can quickly become difficult.

This is where 𝐉𝐢𝐧𝐣𝐚𝟐 𝐓𝐞𝐦𝐩𝐥𝐚𝐭𝐞𝐬 become powerful. 🔥

🔹 𝐖𝐡𝐚𝐭 𝐢𝐬 𝐚 𝐉𝐢𝐧𝐣𝐚𝟐 𝐓𝐞𝐦𝐩𝐥𝐚𝐭𝐞?

A Jinja2 template combines normal configuration with variables and expressions.

Templates usually use the .j2 extension.

Example:

templates/
└── nginx.conf.j2

Inside the template:

server {
    listen {{ nginx_port }};
    server_name {{ server_name }};
    root {{ document_root }};
}

The values inside {{ }} are replaced by Ansible during execution.

🔹 𝐔𝐬𝐢𝐧𝐠 𝐭𝐡𝐞 𝐭𝐞𝐦𝐩𝐥𝐚𝐭𝐞 𝐌𝐨𝐝𝐮𝐥𝐞

- name: Deploy Nginx configuration
  template:
    src: nginx.conf.j2
    dest: /etc/nginx/nginx.conf
  notify: Restart Nginx

Ansible processes the template and creates the final configuration on the target server.

🔹 𝐄𝐱𝐚𝐦𝐩𝐥𝐞 𝐕𝐚𝐫𝐢𝐚𝐛𝐥𝐞𝐬

nginx_port: 8080
server_name: example.com
document_root: /var/www/html

One template can now be reused across multiple environments. 🚀

🌍 𝐑𝐞𝐚𝐥-𝐖𝐨𝐫𝐥𝐝 𝐄𝐱𝐚𝐦𝐩𝐥𝐞

Development → app.example-dev.com
QA → app.example-qa.com
Production → app.example.com

Instead of maintaining three different configuration files, you can maintain one Jinja2 template + different variables.

🔥 𝐉𝐢𝐧𝐣𝐚𝟐 𝐂𝐚𝐧 𝐃𝐨 𝐌𝐨𝐫𝐞

You can use conditions:

{% if environment == "production" %}
server_tokens off;
{% endif %}

And loops:

{% for server in backend_servers %}
server {{ server }};
{% endfor %}

⚠️ 𝐂𝐨𝐦𝐦𝐨𝐧 𝐌𝐢𝐬𝐭𝐚𝐤𝐞𝐬

❌ Incorrect variable names
❌ Wrong YAML indentation
❌ Forgetting the .j2 extension
❌ Using copy when dynamic templating is required

Remember:

𝐜𝐨𝐩𝐲 → 𝐒𝐭𝐚𝐭𝐢𝐜 𝐟𝐢𝐥𝐞𝐬
𝐭𝐞𝐦𝐩𝐥𝐚𝐭𝐞 → 𝐃𝐲𝐧𝐚𝐦𝐢𝐜 𝐟𝐢𝐥𝐞𝐬

🎯 𝐊𝐞𝐲 𝐓𝐚𝐤𝐞𝐚𝐰𝐚𝐲

𝐎𝐧𝐞 𝐭𝐞𝐦𝐩𝐥𝐚𝐭𝐞 + 𝐝𝐢𝐟𝐟𝐞𝐫𝐞𝐧𝐭 𝐯𝐚𝐫𝐢𝐚𝐛𝐥𝐞𝐬 = 𝐫𝐞𝐮𝐬𝐚𝐛𝐥𝐞 𝐚𝐮𝐭𝐨𝐦𝐚𝐭𝐢𝐨𝐧. 🚀

Jinja2 helps separate configuration logic from environment-specific values and makes Infrastructure as Code much more flexible.
